before:
Contains checks whether a value belongs to the set. Count gives the number of distinct members, not the number of attempted additions.

Contains(value) checks membership without adding anything and returns a Boolean. In this separate example:
```csharp
var sample = new System.Collections.Generic.HashSet<int> { 4 };
System.Console.WriteLine(sample.Contains(4));
System.Console.WriteLine(sample.Contains(7));
```
Output: True, then False.

Complete local C# example; the app does not execute it:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
HashSet<int> seen = new HashSet<int>();
seen.Add(4);
bool added = seen.Add(4);
Console.WriteLine(added);
Console.WriteLine(seen.Count);
```

